    "def-moments-variance-and-covariance": {
      "id": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
      "path": "items/def-moments-variance-and-covariance.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "definition",
      "exact_statement": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
      "sha256": "bfd65357a8e762288fd2fd8c257b432aee7153a9972ca6d7a56f9194b9337ef1",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "lem-qft-example-covariant-estimate-errors"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/probability/probability-spaces-random-variables-and-expectation.md",
          "page": "probability-spaces-random-variables-and-expectation",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms": {
      "id": "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
      "path": "items/def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "definition",
      "exact_statement": "Fix an integer $n\\ge1$. With the complex smooth-function convention of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]] and multi-indices of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], define\n$$p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)=\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R^n}|x^\\alpha\\partial^\\beta f(x)|,\\qquad \\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)=\\{f\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C):p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)<\\infty\\text{ for all }\\alpha,\\beta\\}.$$\nThe zero multi-index gives $x^0=1$ and $\\partial^0f=f$. These are actual smooth functions, not equivalence classes. The notation $C_c^\\infty$ uses the support convention in [[def-c-c-and-c-c-infinity-on-rn]].\n\nPointwise differentiation is linear; thus $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f+g)\\le p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)+p_{\\alpha\\beta}(g)$ and $p_{\\alpha\\beta}(cf)=|c|p_{\\alpha\\beta}(f)$. In particular this set is a complex vector space and each $p_{\\alpha\\beta}$ is a seminorm. The zero function belongs to it. Since $p_{00}(f)=0$ forces $f(x)=0$ for every $x$, the family separates functions. No choice principle is needed.",
      "sha256": "5677424683a1a1a258d447c29b9e618258dcb2f46cf67847c5631cdc4a267827",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/functional-analysis/schwartz-space-and-the-plancherel-theorem.md",
          "page": "schwartz-space-and-the-plancherel-theorem",
