        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-fourier-transform-agrees-with-l-one-and-plancherel-transforms": {
      "id": "thm-fourier-transform-agrees-with-l-one-and-plancherel-transforms",
      "path": "items/thm-fourier-transform-agrees-with-l-one-and-plancherel-transforms.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use the negative-sign $2\\pi$ normalization.  If\n$f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, then\n\n$$\\mathcal F u_f=u_{\\widehat f},$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat f$ is the integral Fourier transform.  If\n$f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, then\n\n$$\\mathcal F u_f=u_{\\mathcal F_2f},$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal F_2$ is the Plancherel extension.  These equalities are in\n$\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^n)$ and therefore depend only on the corresponding\nalmost-everywhere classes.",
      "sha256": "0cbba314d6ec7264fb9768012b87bb60af99ce8fdefff0cf1e6d2b638e15c528",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/functional-analysis/tempered-distributions-and-the-fourier-transform.md",
          "page": "tempered-distributions-and-the-fourier-transform",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space": {
      "id": "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space",
      "path": "items/lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space and let\n$L^2(\\mu)$ be the quotient of $\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$ by the almost-everywhere zero\nfunctions ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]]), with the quotient\nnorm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ ([[thm-the-l-p-norm-descends-to-the-quotient-and-makes-l-p-a-normed-space]]).\n\n1. On **real** $L^2(\\mu)$ the formula\n   $$\\langle f,g\\rangle:=\\int fg\\,d\\mu$$is a well-defined inner product, linear in both variables, positive definite, with $\\langle f,f\\rangle=\\|f\\|_2^2$; with it $L^2(\\mu)$ is a real Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]). 2. On **complex** $L^2(\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ the formula$$\\langle f,g\\rangle:=\\int f\\overline g\\,d\\mu$$\n   is a well-defined inner product, linear in the first variable and\n   conjugate-linear in the second, positive definite, with\n   $\\langle f,f\\rangle=\\|f\\|_2^2$; with it $L^2(\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ is a complex\n   Hilbert space ([[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]).\n\nIn both cases the inner product induces exactly the established quotient\n$L^2$ norm.",
      "sha256": "1ce8e8046d06c839a498689ee1f089c2985fc2e6ddb4ed78b82281e8c599ef05",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/functional-analysis/orthonormal-bases-parseval-and-fourier-series.md",
          "page": "orthonormal-bases-parseval-and-fourier-series",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "def-axiom-of-choice": {
      "id": "def-axiom-of-choice",
      "path": "items/def-axiom-of-choice.md",
      "status": "published",
      "domain": "mathematics",
      "kind": "definition",
      "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
      "sha256": "9c045248c142fcf687fdc3c5d25d8b3890ac08d1eca8b4e6d71bb00a4eb6ff58",
      "reading_status": "status/statement extracted; actual proof-reading extent belongs to specialist supplier record",
      "consumers": [
        "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian",
        "lem-qft-test-frechet-uniform-scaling-order"
      ],
      "home_kind": "canonical-root-item",
      "original_page_homes": [
        {
          "path": "library/foundations/relations-functions-and-quotients.md",
          "page": "relations-functions-and-quotients",
          "is_B": false,
          "status": "published"
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-c-c-infinity-rn-is-dense-in-l-p-of-rn": {
      "id": "thm-c-c-infinity-rn-is-dense-in-l-p-of-rn",
      "path": "items/thm-c-c-infinity-rn-is-dense-in-l-p-of-rn.md",
